1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,560
NIO.2 gave us paths and helpers. The
helpers are polite enough that you can
forget an older, sharper lesson: text is
not bytes.

2
00:00:08,679 --> 00:00:14,719
Imagine you open a customer export that
contains names with accents or text
produced on another operating system.

3
00:00:14,839 --> 00:00:26,960
If you read it as raw bytes and casually
turn those bytes into a String with the
wrong encoding, characters break, equality
checks fail, and downstream reports look
haunted. The bug is not in your business
rule.

4
00:00:27,079 --> 00:00:38,879
It is in the boundary between byte streams
and character streams — a boundary Java
models on purpose. So how does Java force
that boundary into the open? Readers and
writers are character streams.

5
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:48,399
Input streams and output streams are byte
streams. When you mean text, prefer
readers and writers — or higher helpers
that still take an explicit charset.

6
00:00:48,519 --> 00:00:55,599
When you mean binary — images, compressed
payloads, length-prefixed frames — stay
with bytes.

7
00:00:55,719 --> 00:01:04,159
Mixing the two without an intentional
decoder is how mojibake enters a codebase
that "only deals with files." Look at this
java for a moment.

8
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:14,240
newBufferedWriter gives you a buffered
Writer tied to a path, with UTF-8 named in
the call. Try-with-resources closes and
flushes according to the resource
contract.

9
00:01:14,359 --> 00:01:23,760
The charset is not buried in platform
defaults — it is part of the method call
you can defend in review. Buffering is not
optional for performance once writes
happen in a loop.

10
00:01:23,879 --> 00:01:31,679
Unbuffered tiny writes — one character at
a time straight to a cold OS call — can
crush throughput on what looks like a
simple export.

11
00:01:31,799 --> 00:01:40,200
You still must understand flush: buffering
means "not necessarily on disk yet." If
another process must see the data before
close, flush intentionally.

12
00:01:40,319 --> 00:01:51,439
Close also flushes for well-behaved
writers, which is another reason try-with-
resources matters. InputStreamReader is
the bridge when you already have bytes and
need characters: Look at this java for a
moment.

13
00:01:51,559 --> 00:02:01,159
Bytes arrive on inputStream.
InputStreamReader decodes them with UTF-8.
BufferedReader makes line-oriented reading
practical. Each layer has one job.

14
00:02:01,279 --> 00:02:11,639
Skipping the charset argument invites the
default-encoding bug back through a side
door — the code looks sophisticated and
still depends on whatever the JVM's
default happens to be today.

15
00:02:11,759 --> 00:02:28,479
What if we ignore the distinction and read
text as raw bytes "because it is simpler"?
Look at this java for a moment. It looks
small. Prefer Files.readString(path,
StandardCharsets.UTF_8) or an explicit
decoding path.

16
00:02:28,599 --> 00:02:41,879
The rule is how text survives the trip
between machines, containers, and
developer laptops. Flush and close
semantics close the lesson. Writers may
hold data in memory. Streams may hold
native resources.

17
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:50,800
Neither flush nor close, and another
reader sees a truncated file. Flush
obsessively after every character, and you
may be correct and still slow.

18
00:02:50,919 --> 00:03:04,039
Buffer by default, flush when a checkpoint
is required, close with try-with-
resources. Another pressure appears in
loops that write many lines. People
sometimes reopen the file in append mode
for each line.

19
00:03:04,159 --> 00:03:12,840
The character-stream model is not only
about encoding — it is about owning one
writer for the lifetime of the export,
buffering it, and closing it once.

20
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:25,960
What if the file is binary after all — a
compressed archive or an image? Then a
Writer is the wrong abstraction. Match the
stream family to the payload, and when the
payload is text, name the charset out
loud.

21
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:35,560
Once encoding is explicit, many "random"
production text bugs stop looking random.
They become missing charset arguments you
can find in review.

22
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:48,520
The same bridge shows up for network
sockets: bytes arrive from the wire;
characters leave into a log or CSV. Prefer
explicit charset in every text boundary
you own — files, sockets, and batch
stdout.

23
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:57,640
Defaults are convenient until they become
an incident. Until now, our programs have
mostly walked one path of execution from
main downward.

24
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:06,640
Real systems often need more than one path
at once — a background worker, a
concurrent request, a timer — and that is
where threads enter the story.
